reak the existing B5-B6-B7 meld, which is bad. \\n\\nDiscarding B8: leaves B7, but no help. \\n\\nDiscarding T7: as discussed, doesn't help. \\n\\nDiscarding F1: leaves F2. Maybe not as beneficial. \\n\\nDiscarding F2: leaves F1. \\n\\nSo between F1 and F2, discarding F2 to keep F1 (South Wind) is better. \\n\\nTherefore, the best move is to discard F2. This way, I keep F1 in hand, which could help with Seat Wind scoring if I can form a Pung. \\n\\nAnother consideration: the unshown tiles for F2 have 1 left. If I discard F2, someone else might have it and use it, but I can't Peng since I only have one. \\n\\nAlternatively, if I keep F2, I have two different Winds in hand (F1 and F2), which might not help much. \\n\\nSo the best choice is to discard F2.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: [Discarding F2 allows keeping F1 (South Wind) in hand, which aligns with Seat Wind scoring. It also maintains potential for forming additional melds in the T4-T5-T6-T7 sequence without breaking existing melds.]\\nReason: [Discarding F2 preserves the possibility of scoring points for Seat Wind (South Wind) and avoids breaking the B5-B6-B7 meld. It also keeps the T4-T5-T6-T7 sequence intact for potential future meld formation.]\\nAnswer: Play F2\"}"}
