raw T8. But if they keep T6, they can. Similarly for T7.\n\nB7 is part of the B5-B6-B7 chow. Discarding it would prevent forming B7-B8-B9, but B8 is already out (unshown B8 has 0 left). So no help there.\n\nW7 is a single tile. If the user keeps it, they need two more to form a pung. But there are two left. So possible.\n\nSo the options are:\n\n- Discard W7: Lose the chance to form a pung of W7. But W7 is not part of any existing chow. However, keeping it might help if they can get two more.\n\n- Discard T6: Lose the chance to form T6-T7-T8 if T8 is available. T8 has 1 left. So possible.\n\n- Discard T7: Lose the chance to form T5-T6-T7 (already exists) or T7-T8-T9. T8 has 1 left, T9 has 1 left. So possible.\n\n- Discard B7: No real benefit.\n\n- Discard F4: Lose the chance to form a pung of F4 (prevailing wind).\n\nBetween these, the most valuable is probably keeping F4 for the prevailing wind pung. So discarding one of the other tiles. Which one?\n\nIf the user discards B7, it's the least useful. So maybe that's the best option. Alternatively, discarding W7, since it's not part of any chow.\n\nBut the valid actions are Play W7; Play F4; Play T6; Play B7; Play T7.\n\nSo the options are to play any of these. Which is the best choice?\n\nIf the user plays F4, they give up the chance to form a pung of F4 (prevailing wind), which is worth 2 points. That's significant. So maybe it's better to keep F4.\n\nIf they play B7, they lose the chance to form B7-B8-B9, but B8 is already out. So not useful. So playing B7 is better than playing F4, because F4 is more valuable.\n\nIf they play T6 or T7, they might lose the chance to form new chows. For example, T6 could help in T4-T5-T6 or T6-T7-T8. T7 could help in T7-T8-T9.\n\nComparing all options, playing B7 seems the least impactful. So the best move is to play B7.\n\nBut wait, the user's melds already include B5-B6-B7. So B7 is part of that chow. If the user is holding another B7, maybe they have a pung of B7? Wait, the unshown B7 has 2 left. The user has one in hand. So if they have another B7, they could form a pung. But the meld is a chow of B5-B6-B7. So if the user has another B7, they could form a pung if they get two more. But the meld is already a chow. So the user has one B7 in hand. If they discard it, they lose the chance to form a pung of B7. But the chow is already formed. So maybe it's better to keep it for possible pung. However, since the user already has a chow of B5-B6-B7, having another B7 could allow a pung if they get two more. But there are two B7s left. So possible. But the user has one in hand. If they discard it, they can't form that pung. So maybe it's better to keep B7.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let's try to summarize:\n\n- Playing F4: Risk losing a potential pung of F4 (prevailing wind, 2 points).\n\n- Playing B7: Risk losing a potential pung of B7 (if they can get two more).\n\n- Playing T6 or T7: Risk losing potential chows.\n\nGiven that F4 is the prevailing wind and a pung would give 2 points, it's better to keep F4. Between B7, T6, and T7, which is better to discard?\n\nIf the user discards B7, they lose the chance to form a pung of B7. But the chow is already formed. So maybe it's less critical. T6 and T7 are part of the existing chow and could help form new ones. So discarding T6 or T7 might be worse.\n\nSo the best option is to play B7, as it's the least useful for future melds.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to play B7 to avoid giving up potential meld opportunities while preserving more strategic tiles.  \nReason: B7 is part of an already completed chow and has limited potential for new melds (B8 is exhausted). Discarding it allows retaining W7, T6/T7 for possible chows and F4 for a prevailing wind pung (2 points). Other tiles like F4 and T6/T7 are more valuable for scoring.  \nAnswer: Play B7"}
